\chapter{Library and processor search paths}
\label{ch:33}
\index{Library and processor search paths}

Ordinary libraries, standard-library roots, PPXs, and kernel roots have different discovery settings. \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_LIB\_PATH}, \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_STDLIB\_PATH}, \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_PPX\_PATH}, and \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_STDPPX\_PATH} affect the compiler or its build wrapper. \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_MULIB\_PATH} is a build and inspection setting. The equivalent flags accept colon-separated lists and ignore empty entries. Relative entries follow the invoking process's working directory. The legacy \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_PATH} uses its documented host-specific separator.

\section{Reproduce resolution}
For a duplicate package problem, invoke the compiler under the same working directory with explicit root flags. Capture the dependency report and compare selected canonical files before looking at their declarations. For a PPX, also confirm that the processor is activated and that the name maps to the expected executable and manifest. For a kernel, resolve the header independently and inspect its build plan. A discovered name can still fail subsequently because of an unavailable processor, incompatible package, or unsupported ABI.

\section{Resolve three similar-looking requests}
From the project directory, pass \OnxCodeTerm{--lib-path local::vendor}. The entry source lives under \OnxCodeTerm{app/}. Both library directories contain a plain \OnxCodeTerm{rates.on1x}. For \OnxCodeTerm{import rates;} first inspect the directory containing the entry. If it has no candidate, look in the project's \OnxCodeTerm{local/}, then in its \OnxCodeTerm{vendor/}. The double colon adds no third candidate. Moving the entry file while leaving the invocation directory fixed changes the first lookup but not the meaning of those two relative path entries. Changing the working directory can have the opposite effect.

Now change the import to \OnxCodeTerm{std.Int}. A loose \OnxCodeTerm{Int.on1x} beside the entry does not satisfy that request. Ask \OnxCodeTerm{--print-stdlib} which root heads the standard list; use the dependency listing to see the file actually used, since a missing module in that root may be present in a fallback. A manifested standard library can enter the list through a general library directory.

Finally suppose two processor directories advertise the same name. The dedicated standard processor search list precedes the general one. If the first matching discovery manifest is malformed, removing the bad directory and retrying can change the outcome; the original lookup does not silently skip the error. Here the evidence to collect is the processor manifest and its relative executable path. A successful ordinary module dependency listing cannot tell you which executable would run on activation.

For kernel lookup, the corresponding evidence is the selected header path and hash in the plan. Finding a header with the requested basename need not reproduce a previous build's header contents.

